{
  "nbformat": 4,
  "nbformat_minor": 5,
  "metadata": {
    "kernelspec": {
      "name": "maxima",
      "display_name": "Maxima",
      "language": "maxima"
    },
    "aximar": {}
  },
  "cells": [
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "# Quantum Wells and the Schrödinger Equation\n\nThis notebook finds the bound states of a particle in four one-dimensional potentials: the infinite square well, the harmonic oscillator, the finite square well and the double well. One numerical method covers all four. Replacing the second derivative with a finite difference turns the Schrödinger equation into a matrix eigenvalue problem, and `np_eig` returns the energies and wavefunctions together.\n\nThe first three potentials have exact solutions, which we derive symbolically and use to check the matrix method, including its second-order convergence as the grid is refined. The double well has no closed form; there the matrix method shows the tunnelling splitting between the two lowest states and how it shrinks exponentially as the barrier grows. We use units with $\\hbar = m = 1$ throughout.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "load(\"numerics\")$\nload(\"ax-plots\")$\nload(\"orthopoly\")$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 1,
      "outputs": []
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## The Schrödinger Equation on a Grid [S]\n\nA stationary state $\\psi(x)$ with energy $E$ satisfies the time-independent Schrödinger equation\n\n$$-\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\psi''(x) + V(x)\\,\\psi(x) = E\\,\\psi(x).$$\n\nOn a grid $x_i = x_0 + i h$ the second-difference stencil approximates $\\psi''$. Substituting the Taylor series of $u(x \\pm h)$ into the stencil shows the leading error is proportional to $h^2$, so halving the spacing should cut the error in each energy by a factor of four.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "shifted(s) := sum(diff(u(x), x, j)*(s*h)^j/j!, j, 0, 5)$\nstencil : expand((shifted(1) - 2*u(x) + shifted(-1))/h^2);",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 2,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "{{h^2\\,\\left({{d^4}\\over{d\\,x^4}}\\,u\\left(x\\right)\\right)}\\over{12}}+{{d^2 }\\over{d\\,x^2}}\\,u\\left(x\\right)"
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 2,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Applying the stencil at every interior point, with $\\psi = 0$ at both ends of the grid, gives the matrix equation $H\\boldsymbol\\psi = E\\boldsymbol\\psi$. The kinetic term contributes $1/h^2$ on the diagonal and $-1/(2h^2)$ on the two neighbouring diagonals; the potential adds $V_i = V(x_i)$ to the diagonal. For four interior points:",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "H4 : genmatrix(lambda([i, j],\n       if i = j then 1/h^2 + V[i] elseif abs(i - j) = 1 then -1/(2*h^2) else 0), 4, 4);",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 3,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "\\ifx\\endpmatrix\\undefined\\pmatrix{\\else\\begin{pmatrix}\\fi {{1}\\over{h^2}}+V _{1}&-\\left({{1}\\over{2\\,h^2}}\\right)&0&0\\cr -\\left({{1}\\over{2\\,h^2}}\\right) &{{1}\\over{h^2}}+V_{2}&-\\left({{1}\\over{2\\,h^2}}\\right)&0\\cr 0&-\\left({{1 }\\over{2\\,h^2}}\\right)&{{1}\\over{h^2}}+V_{3}&-\\left({{1}\\over{2\\,h^2}}\\right) \\cr 0&0&-\\left({{1}\\over{2\\,h^2}}\\right)&{{1}\\over{h^2}}+V_{4}\\cr \\ifx\\endpmatrix\\undefined}\\else\\end{pmatrix}\\fi "
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 3,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "The numeric version builds the same tridiagonal matrix as an ndarray. `np_eig` calls LAPACK's general eigensolver, which returns the eigenvalues in no particular order and each eigenvector as a column of unit length. `eig_lowest` sorts the eigenvalues with `np_argsort` and returns the lowest `m` energies with their eigenvectors. The check below runs it on a 200-point well and prints the first few raw eigenvalues, which come out far from sorted, and the norm of one column.\n\nEach eigenvector $\\mathbf v$ has $\\sum_i v_i^2 = 1$, while a normalised wavefunction has $\\int \\psi^2\\,dx \\approx h\\sum_i \\psi_i^2 = 1$. So $\\psi_i = v_i/\\sqrt h$. The overall sign of an eigenvector is arbitrary. For plots `fix_sign` makes each wavefunction rise from the left edge; for comparison with an exact solution `align_sign` flips a vector whose overlap with the exact one is negative.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "grid(lo, hi, N) := block([h : float((hi - lo)/(N + 1))], [h, makelist(lo + i*h, i, 1, N)])$\n\nfd_hamiltonian(h, Vlist) := block([n : length(Vlist), H, off : -0.5/h^2],\n  H : np_diag(makelist(1.0/h^2 + Vlist[i], i, 1, n)),\n  for i : 0 thru n - 2 do (np_set(H, i, i + 1, off), np_set(H, i + 1, i, off)),\n  H)$\n\neig_lowest(H, m) := block([ev, vecs, order],\n  [ev, vecs] : np_eig(H),\n  order : firstn(map(round, np_to_list(np_argsort(ev))), m),\n  [makelist(np_ref(ev, i), i, order), makelist(np_to_list(np_col(vecs, i)), i, order)])$\n\n[h_t, x_t] : grid(0, 1, 200)$\n[ev_t, vecs_t] : np_eig(fd_hamiltonian(h_t, makelist(0.0, i, 1, 200)))$\nprintf(true, \"First raw eigenvalues: ~{~,1f ~}~%\", firstn(np_to_list(ev_t), 4))$\nprintf(true, \"Lowest eigenvalue sits at index ~d~%\", round(np_ref(np_argsort(ev_t), 0)))$\nprintf(true, \"Norm of column 0: ~,12f~%\", sqrt(np_sum(np_pow(np_col(vecs_t, 0), 2))))$\n\nfix_sign(v) := block([vmax : lmax(map(abs, v)), k : 1],\n  while abs(v[k]) < 1e-3*vmax do k : k + 1,\n  if v[k] < 0 then -v else v)$\nto_psi(v, h) := fix_sign(v)/sqrt(h)$\nalign_sign(p, q) := if apply(\"+\", p*q) < 0 then -p else p$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 4,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "First raw eigenvalues: 80797.1 80782.3 80757.6 80723.1\nLowest eigenvalue sits at index 100\nNorm of column 0: 1.000000000000"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## Infinite Square Well [S+N]\n\nInside a box $0 < x < L$ the potential is zero and the walls force $\\psi(0) = \\psi(L) = 0$. With $E = k^2/2$ the equation becomes $\\psi'' = -k^2\\psi$. `ode2` gives the general solution and the left wall removes the cosine.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "assume(k > 0, L > 0)$\ngen_sol : ode2('diff(u, x, 2) = -k^2*u, u, x)$\nleft_bc : solve(subst(x = 0, rhs(gen_sol)) = 0, %k2)$\nu_box : subst(left_bc, rhs(gen_sol))$\n[gen_sol, u = u_box];",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 5,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "\\left[ u={\\it \\%k}_{1}\\,\\sin \\left(k\\,x\\right)+{\\it \\%k}_{2}\\,\\cos \\left(k\\, x\\right) , u={\\it \\%k}_{1}\\,\\sin \\left(k\\,x\\right) \\right] "
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 5,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "The right wall needs $\\sin kL = 0$, so $k = n\\pi/L$ and $E_n = n^2\\pi^2/(2L^2)$. Normalising fixes the amplitude at $\\sqrt{2/L}$. The last line confirms that $\\psi_n$ satisfies the Schrödinger equation with energy $E_n$ for every integer $n$.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "declare(n, integer)$\nassume(n > 0)$\nnorm_sq : integrate(subst(k = n*%pi/L, u_box/%k1)^2, x, 0, L)$\npsi_box(n, x) := sqrt(2/L)*sin(n*%pi*x/L)$\nE_box(n) := n^2*%pi^2/(2*L^2)$\n['norm_sq = norm_sq, 'residual = ratsimp(-diff(psi_box(n, x), x, 2)/2 - E_box(n)*psi_box(n, x))];",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 6,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "text/latex": [
              "\\left[ {\\it norm\\_sq}={{L}\\over{2}} , {\\it residual}=0 \\right] "
            ]
          },
          "execution_count": 6,
          "metadata": {},
          "output_type": "execute_result"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "For $L = 1$ the matrix on 200 interior points reproduces the lowest five levels. The relative error grows with $n$ because higher states oscillate faster and the grid resolves them less well; the stencil's $h^2\\psi''''/12$ term predicts a relative error of $-(n\\pi h)^2/12$.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "[h_box, x_box] : grid(0, 1, 200)$\n[E_fd, v_fd] : eig_lowest(fd_hamiltonian(h_box, makelist(0.0, i, 1, 200)), 5)$\nprintf(true, \" n   exact E     matrix E    rel. error   predicted~%\")$\nfor j : 1 thru 5 do block([ex : float(subst(L = 1, E_box(j)))],\n  printf(true, \"~2d ~11,5f ~11,5f ~12,3e ~12,3e~%\",\n         j, ex, E_fd[j], (E_fd[j] - ex)/ex, -(j*%pi*h_box)^2/12))$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 7,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            " n   exact E     matrix E    rel. error   predicted\n 1     4.93480     4.93470    -2.036e-5    -2.036e-5\n 2    19.73921    19.73760    -8.143e-5    -8.143e-5\n 3    44.41322    44.40508    -1.832e-4    -1.832e-4\n 4    78.95684    78.93112    -3.257e-4    -3.257e-4\n 5   123.37006   123.30728    -5.088e-4    -5.089e-4"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "Doubling the number of grid points roughly halves $h$, so the error should fall by about four each time. The last column gives the ratio of successive errors for the ground state.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "box_errors(N) := block([g : grid(0, 1, N), Es],\n  Es : first(eig_lowest(fd_hamiltonian(g[1], makelist(0.0, i, 1, N)), 3)),\n  makelist(abs(Es[j] - float(subst(L = 1, E_box(j))))/float(subst(L = 1, E_box(j))), j, 1, 3))$\nprev : false$\nprintf(true, \"   N    err n=1     err n=2     err n=3     ratio n=1~%\")$\nfor N in [50, 100, 200, 400, 800] do block([e : box_errors(N)],\n  printf(true, \"~4d ~11,3e ~11,3e ~11,3e   ~a~%\", N, e[1], e[2], e[3],\n         if prev = false then \"\" else printf(false, \"~,3f\", prev/e[1])),\n  prev : e[1])$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 8,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "   N    err n=1     err n=2     err n=3     ratio n=1\n  50    3.162e-4    1.264e-3    2.843e-3\n 100    8.062e-5    3.225e-4    7.254e-4   3.922\n 200    2.036e-5    8.143e-5    1.832e-4   3.960\n 400    5.115e-6    2.046e-5    4.603e-5   3.980\n 800    1.282e-6    5.128e-6    1.154e-5   3.990"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "The ratio settles at 4, the signature of a second-order method. The figure shows the textbook picture: each wavefunction drawn on its own energy level, with the box walls in black.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "source": "palette : [\"#1f77b4\", \"#ff7f0e\", \"#2ca02c\", \"#d62728\", \"#9467bd\"]$\n\nlevel_objects(xs, Vl, E, psis, scale) := block([objs : [], xa],\n  for j : 1 thru length(E) do (\n    xa : sublist(makelist(i, i, 1, length(xs)), lambda([i], Vl[i] <= E[j])),\n    objs : append(objs, [\n      lines([xs[first(xa)], xs[last(xa)]], [E[j], E[j]],\n            color = \"gray\", dash = \"dot\", line_width = 1, showlegend = false),\n      lines(xs, E[j] + scale*psis[j], color = palette[j], dash = \"solid\",\n            line_width = 2, name = sconcat(\"n = \", j - 1 + n_first))])),\n  objs)$\n\nn_first : 1$\npsi_box_fd : makelist(to_psi(v_fd[j], h_box), j, 1, 4)$\nax_draw2d(\n  level_objects(x_box, makelist(0.0, i, 1, 200), firstn(E_fd, 4), psi_box_fd, 6.0),\n  lines([0, 0, 1, 1], [90, 0, 0, 90], color = \"black\", line_width = 3, name = \"V(x)\"),\n  title = \"Infinite Square Well: Levels and Wavefunctions\",\n  xlabel = \"x\", ylabel = \"E, and ψ offset by E\", xrange = [-0.1, 1.1], yrange = [-2, 90])$",
      "metadata": {},
      "execution_count": 9,
      "outputs": [
        {
          "data": {
            "application/x-maxima-plotly": [
              "{\"data\":[{\"x\":[0.004975124378109453,0.9950248756218906],\"y\":[4.934701740696799,4.934701740696799],\"type\":\"scatter\",\"mode\":\"lines\",\"line\":{\"color\":\"gray\",\"width\":1,\"dash\":\"dot\"},\"showlegend\":false,\"name\":\"lines\"},{\"x\":[0.004975124378109453,0.009950248756218905,0.014925373134328358,0.01990049751243781,0.02487562189054726,0.029850746268656716,0.03482587064676617,0.03980099502487562,0.04477611940298507,0.04975124378109452,0.05472636815920398,0.05970149253731343,0.06467661691542288,0.06965174129353234,0.07462686567164178,0.07960199004975124,0.0845771144278607,0.08955223880597014,0.0945273631840796,0.09950248756218905,0.1044776119402985,0.10945273631840796,0.1144278606965174,0.11940298507462686,0.12437810945273632,0.12935323383084577,0.13432835820895522,0.13930348258706468,0.14427860696517414,0.14925373134328357,0.15422885572139303,0.15920398009950248,0.16417910447761194,0.1691542288557214,0.17412935323383083,0.1791044776119403,0.18407960199004975,0.1890547263681592,0.19402985074626866,0.1990049751243781,0.20398009950248755,0.208955223880597,0.21393034825870647,0.21890547263681592,0.22388059701492538,0.2288557213930348,0.23383084577114427,0.23880597014925373,0.24378109452736318,0.24875621890547264,0.2537313432835821,0.25870646766169153,0.263681592039801,0.26865671641791045,0.2736318407960199,0.27860696517412936,0.2835820895522388,0.2885572139303483,0.2935323383084577,0.29850746268656714,0.3034825870646766,0.30845771144278605,0.31343283582089554,0.31840796019900497,0.3233830845771144,0.3283582089552239,0.3333333333333333,0.3383084577114428,0.34328358208955223,0.34825870646766166,0.35323383084577115,0.3582089552238806,0.36318407960199006,0.3681592039800995,0.3731343283582089,0.3781094527363184,0.38308457711442784,0.3880597014925373,0.39303482587064675,0.3980099502487562,0.40298507462686567,0.4079601990049751,0.4129353233830846,0.417910447761194,0.4228855721393035,0.42786069651741293,0.43283582089552236,0.43781094527363185,0.4