    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "The splitting drops by more than two orders of magnitude while the barrier rises eightfold, and on the logarithmic axis the two curves run nearly parallel. The ratio climbs towards 1, and $(1 - \\text{ratio})\\,S_0$ stays between 1.0 and 1.3 and drifts slowly down, as an expansion in $1/S_0$ predicts.\n\n## Summary\n\n| Step | Method |\n|------|--------|\n| Discretisation | Symbolic: Taylor expansion of the second-difference stencil, error $h^2\\psi''''/12$ |\n| Hamiltonian matrix | Tridiagonal ndarray; `np_eig` with eigenvalues sorted by `np_argsort` |\n| Infinite square well | `ode2` and boundary conditions give $n^2\\pi^2/(2L^2)$; error ratio 4 per grid doubling |\n| Harmonic oscillator | `hermite` eigenfunctions checked symbolically; matrix energies and normalised wavefunctions agree |\n| Finite square well | Matching conditions solved with `find_root`; grid aligned with the well edges |\n| Double well | Tunnelling splitting from the two lowest eigenvalues, compared with the instanton formula |\n\nThe matrix method needs only the potential on a grid, so `fd_hamiltonian` and `eig_lowest` handle any other one-dimensional potential unchanged.",
      "metadata": {}
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "source": "## References\n\n- D. Tong, *Lectures on Quantum Mechanics*, §2.3.1 *A Finite Potential Well*: the even-state condition $k \\tan ka = \\eta$, eq. (2.36), and its odd counterpart.\n- L. D. Landau and E. M. Lifshitz, *Quantum Mechanics*, §50, Problem 3: the splitting of a level in a symmetric double well in the quasi-classical approximation, $\\Delta E = (\\hbar\\omega/\\pi)\\exp(-\\frac{1}{\\hbar}\\int |p|\\,dx)$. For the wells here it agrees with the instanton formula to within 4%.\n- R. Shankar, *Principles of Quantum Mechanics*, §21.2: tunnelling in the double well from the imaginary-time path integral, the origin of the instanton formula's $e^{-S_0}$.",
      "metadata": {}
    }
  ]
}